BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (  The villages discovered that their prosperity depended not on a single perfect exchange, but o) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    n the rhythm they created together. Some mornings, the upstream village sent generously-four) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (     or five seeds-trusting in the river's magic and their neighbors' hearts. Other times, they ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (    sent three, conserving wisely while still feeding the flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (  The downstream village learned to read these offerings as messages. When much came, they retur) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (    ned proportionally, honoring the sender's courage. When less came, they still returned fairl) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (    y, understanding that caution has its place alongside faith.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (  An old weaver taught her grandchildren: "The river's power lies not in one grand gesture, but ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (    in the constancy of the current. Send with hope, return with honor. Neither village can flou) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (    rish alone-we are bound by the water that runs between us.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (  Through droughts and floods, the rhythm persisted. Some exchanges were more generous than othe) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (    rs, but the villages maintained their covenant: to keep the river alive, to trust without fo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    olishness, to return without greed. And so both villages grew strong, not through perfect tr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (    ansactions, but through the patient weaving of reciprocity.) Tj ET
